# Cloudflare Pages response headers. This directory is the deploy root, so
# this file lands at /_headers and applies from the next deploy.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why everything revalidates
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# None of the assets here are fingerprinted - they are app.js, style.css,
# _tokens.css, data/entries.json, by those exact names. That single fact
# decides the policy, because max-age on an unfingerprinted file is a promise
# that cannot be withdrawn: no purge, redeploy or cache-clear reaches a
# browser that has already stored it. The number is therefore not "how long is
# this file likely to stay valid" but "how long am I prepared for a broken
# deploy to stay live for someone who has already visited". Zero.
#
# Pages defaulted to max-age=14400 for static assets while serving the HTML at
# max-age=0, which let a visitor pair four-hour-old JavaScript with current
# markup and a current dictionary. Those three are not independent: entries.json
# and app.js ship as a matched pair (the etymologyMorphemes `analysis` field
# and its renderer went out together, as did chosenBy and siblingEntryIds).
# The renderer tolerates old data with new code by design; nothing guards the
# reverse. Putting every file on one policy means a visitor cannot hold a
# mixed set.
#
# What it costs, measured against production: a 304 carries no body and about
# 715 bytes of headers, ~100ms, multiplexed onto the connection already open
# for the HTML. The three assets revalidate in parallel, so a warm cache pays
# roughly one extra round trip - and a cold cache pays that anyway, since it
# has to fetch them regardless.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The service worker holds the same line
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# public/sw.js caches the shell and the data so the dictionary works offline,
# and a service-worker cache has no expiry at all - which is the problem above
# with the volume turned up. So it does not get to decide for itself when its
# copy is stale: the cache is named after data/version.json's build stamp, that
# stamp is fetched from the network on every activation, and a changed stamp
# throws the whole set away rather than refreshing part of it. Never a partial
# update, for the same reason as everything above: app.js and entries.json ship
# as a matched pair.
#
# sw.js and data/version.json are covered by the /* rule below, which is what
# has to happen - a cached service worker or a cached build stamp is a cache
# that cannot be told it is wrong.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# When to change this
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# If repeat-visit latency ever matters enough to want real caching, lengthen
# the window only *after* fingerprinting the filenames, never before:
#
#   /app.<hash>.js, /style.<hash>.css   Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
#   /                                   Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
#
# That gives zero network on repeat visits and instant invalidation, because a
# new deploy references new URLs. It needs the build to stamp the hash into
# index.html, which is the frontend build step the README currently avoids.
# The no-build-step version - app.js?v=2026-08-03, bumped by hand - works
# until the bump is forgotten, at which point the stale copy is
# indistinguishable from a bug.
#
# Do not give data/* a longer life or a stale-while-revalidate window on its
# own, tempting though it is at 1.4 MB: it decouples the dictionary from the
# code that reads it, which is the mismatch this policy exists to prevent.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Order matters here, and not in the way it reads
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pages does not pick one matching rule, it applies every matching rule and
# appends. With /fonts/* written above /*, both matched and the fonts went out
# with the two glued together:
#
#   Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable, public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
#
# Two max-ages in one header is ambiguous - most browsers take the first, not
# all of them do, and immutable next to must-revalidate is a contradiction on
# its face. So the general rule goes first, and the fonts rule detaches what it
# set before setting its own. `! Cache-Control` is the Pages syntax for that.
#
# This only became visible once the Cloudflare dashboard stopped rewriting
# these. Browser Cache TTL on the domain was set to 4 hours, which replaced the
# max-age on every file Cloudflare edge-caches - css, js and fonts - while
# leaving html and json alone, so the file below was being obeyed for exactly
# the half of the site nobody would think to check. It is set to "Respect
# Existing Headers" now. If these values ever stop matching what is served,
# look there before looking here.

/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The one exception, and the file above explains why it is allowed to be one:
# lengthen the window only after fingerprinting the filenames. These are
# fingerprinted, and not by us - they keep the names Google generated, which are
# content hashes. A different cut of a font is a different filename, so a stale
# copy cannot be served under a name that now means something else, which is the
# whole failure the max-age=0 policy exists to prevent. Nothing else in the
# project qualifies: app.js, style.css and data/entries.json are those names
# forever.
#
# Worth having on its own terms: sw.js caches the shell, but not these, so
# without this every repeat visit revalidates four font files that cannot have
# changed.
/fonts/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
